  "body": "Found by the high-tier review of PR #1306 (2026-11-12, its review comment, non-blocking note 1). It exists on main today; #1306 widens it slightly.\n\n**What happens.** Reach law (c3) judges the tool stack (shank, holder bands) during a turn between two poses by sampling 5 poses and reading each band's column at each sample. Between samples the band moves SIDEWAYS out of every sampled column, and on large turns there are also gaps in Z.\n\n**Readings (reviewer's throwaway probe, brute-force sampled bands, 1500 random turns):**\n- sideways: the band leaves all sampled columns in 665 of 1500 turns - the same on main and on #1306;\n- Z gaps: 11 of 1500 turns on main, 27 on #1306 (16 only there, because the old whole-radius drop used to hide them); worst 1.8 mm, on A/B turns of +-45 to +-85 degrees; turns of +-15 degrees have none.\n\n**What still protects.** The finished part is covered by the solid law, which places the real pose. Leftover STOCK (rest material) and anything else only the column laws read is not: a holder or shank can sweep through rest stock mid-turn without a `ShankHit` / `HolderHit`.\n\nRated `P1` (a collision that can pass verify). If tracing shows another law does convict every such case (e.g. the posed sweep already covers stock for these moves), say so with a test and re-rate.\n\n**Expected.** The mid-turn reading bounds the whole swept band between two poses, not five snapshots: either a swept enclosure per interval (as the posed fixture sweep does since PR #1307 - swing pad from the rim's distance, covering every pose inside the interval), or a sample count derived from the turn angle and band size with a stated bound on what lies between samples. One reading, shared with the posed sweep where possible.\n\nDone means: a brute-force probe like the reviewer's (random turns, bands sampled densely) finds zero uncovered band positions, in XY and in Z, pinned as a test; the existing mid-turn tests stay green without loosening; false convictions the wider reading brings are counted and recorded in the decision note.\n",
